WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.370 --> 00:00:08.931
I'm excited to talk about making
PowerPoint presentations with R

00:00:08.931 --> 00:00:09.555
Markdown today.

00:00:09.555 --> 00:00:11.940
This is a project
that has been--

00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:13.900
we've been working
on for a while now.

00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:18.030
And it's nice to finally
present some of the material.

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:24.390
So you're probably
wondering why would you

00:00:24.390 --> 00:00:27.440
want to create a PowerPoint
presentation from R Markdown.

00:00:30.810 --> 00:00:32.910
Well, there's a
number of reasons,

00:00:32.910 --> 00:00:34.770
but I'm going to
point out a few.

00:00:34.770 --> 00:00:37.965
So one reason is that
everyone knows PowerPoint.

00:00:37.965 --> 00:00:40.090
It's a standard communication
tool, especially used

00:00:40.090 --> 00:00:41.070
in business.

00:00:41.070 --> 00:00:44.100
People learn to speak the
language of PowerPoint.

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:45.540
And the fact of
the matter is it's

00:00:45.540 --> 00:00:47.070
very useful because it works.

00:00:47.070 --> 00:00:49.980
It's very flexible, you
can make slides fast,

00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:52.630
and everyone has personal
experience with it.

00:00:52.630 --> 00:00:53.740
Some people love it.

00:00:53.740 --> 00:00:56.670
Some people not so much but
everyone has an opinion on it

00:00:56.670 --> 00:00:58.170
because we've all
touched this tool.

00:01:01.030 --> 00:01:04.510
But your work, of
course, is coded in R.

00:01:04.510 --> 00:01:08.220
And that's because your
code is your product.

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:10.120
So your output is
a manifestation

00:01:10.120 --> 00:01:13.000
of your codes of plots,
tables, model output, that's

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:15.550
all coming from your code.

00:01:15.550 --> 00:01:18.430
Well that's where R Markdown
becomes very useful.

00:01:18.430 --> 00:01:20.560
R Markdown makes your
work reproducible.

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:23.290
You can weave text and
code to produce elegantly

00:01:23.290 --> 00:01:24.700
formatted output.

00:01:24.700 --> 00:01:26.800
You can use things
like R Notebooks

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:28.890
for doing interactive analysis.

00:01:28.890 --> 00:01:32.230
R Markdown, if you
haven't used it, is--

00:01:32.230 --> 00:01:34.990
will allow you to create a
number of different output

00:01:34.990 --> 00:01:35.860
types--

00:01:35.860 --> 00:01:40.120
reports, apps, dashboards, HTML
docs, PDFs, Word documents,

00:01:40.120 --> 00:01:43.880
but not PowerPoint,
not until now.

00:01:43.880 --> 00:01:47.150
So now you can make PowerPoint
presentations with R Markdown.

00:01:47.150 --> 00:01:50.690
As of RStudio version 1.2,
you can render PowerPoint

00:01:50.690 --> 00:01:53.090
presentations from the IDE.

00:01:53.090 --> 00:01:56.101
This combines two great things,
PowerPoint and R Markdown.

00:01:56.101 --> 00:01:58.100
You can think of it as
the Reese's peanut butter

00:01:58.100 --> 00:02:00.192
cup of presentation software.

00:02:00.192 --> 00:02:01.775
I'm not sure which
one's the chocolate

00:02:01.775 --> 00:02:03.900
and which one's
the peanut butter,

00:02:03.900 --> 00:02:06.830
but you can tell I put this
together during the Halloween

00:02:06.830 --> 00:02:09.210
time period.

00:02:09.210 --> 00:02:12.276
So let me show you a quick
demo of doing PowerPoint

00:02:12.276 --> 00:02:13.400
presentations with RStudio.

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:25.315
So this is RStudio
preview version 1.2.

00:02:25.315 --> 00:02:33.960
If I go up to file,
new file R Markdown

00:02:33.960 --> 00:02:39.450
and then I choose presentation
down at the bottom,

00:02:39.450 --> 00:02:41.490
I have an option
the new PowerPoint.

00:02:41.490 --> 00:02:44.919
I click OK, and this is
an R Markdown document.

00:02:44.919 --> 00:02:47.210
Again, if you've never seen
R Markdown, it's going to--

00:02:47.210 --> 00:02:51.300
it will contain the
pros, some narrative,

00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:53.220
plus some R code
chunks, some things

00:02:53.220 --> 00:02:56.200
about R that you might want
to include in your document.

00:02:56.200 --> 00:03:00.674
And then the output type here
is PowerPoint presentation.

00:03:00.674 --> 00:03:03.090
And the way you create the
PowerPoint presentations is you

00:03:03.090 --> 00:03:05.700
hit this knit button.

00:03:05.700 --> 00:03:07.227
I want to hit this.

00:03:07.227 --> 00:03:08.060
I'll give it a name.

00:03:12.820 --> 00:03:16.630
And it generates
a PowerPoint deck

00:03:16.630 --> 00:03:22.050
with the content, some
bullets, some R code output,

00:03:22.050 --> 00:03:25.190
and a nice little plot.

00:03:25.190 --> 00:03:28.900
Now if you don't want to use
the RStudio ID, that's fine.

00:03:28.900 --> 00:03:30.900
You don't have to
use the RStudio ID.

00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:33.600
You can always render
this programmatically.

00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:35.760
This all works with
open source software.

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:38.610
You can do this inside
of an R console.

00:03:38.610 --> 00:03:41.800
Type in render, give it the
name and the output format,

00:03:41.800 --> 00:03:44.550
and that will also generate
the PowerPoint output.

00:03:55.240 --> 00:03:58.700
So some of the benefits
of using R Markdown here.

00:03:58.700 --> 00:04:00.550
Well, in this book,
R for Data Science,

00:04:00.550 --> 00:04:02.740
there's this lovely chapter
at the end of this book.

00:04:02.740 --> 00:04:05.590
If you haven't seen it, I
recommend that you read it.

00:04:05.590 --> 00:04:09.490
It's about R Markdown about
communicating insights

00:04:09.490 --> 00:04:12.250
to decision makers,
and it makes the point

00:04:12.250 --> 00:04:16.779
that R Markdown is a great
way to influence other people

00:04:16.779 --> 00:04:18.790
in your organization
and other people that

00:04:18.790 --> 00:04:21.250
will be making decisions
based on the work

00:04:21.250 --> 00:04:23.285
that you do in
your data science.

00:04:23.285 --> 00:04:25.810
It will allow you to do better
data science because you'll

00:04:25.810 --> 00:04:28.300
be collaborating with
other data scientists who

00:04:28.300 --> 00:04:30.700
are able to read your
code, understand your code,

00:04:30.700 --> 00:04:32.342
and reproduce your code.

00:04:32.342 --> 00:04:34.300
And it gives you this
nice environment in which

00:04:34.300 --> 00:04:35.860
you can do data science.

00:04:35.860 --> 00:04:38.050
When you put your
narrative together

00:04:38.050 --> 00:04:41.920
with the code and the output
into a single document,

00:04:41.920 --> 00:04:43.810
I would argue through
personal experience

00:04:43.810 --> 00:04:45.880
that you do better data science.

00:04:45.880 --> 00:04:48.910
It allows you to think more
clearly and understand why

00:04:48.910 --> 00:04:50.880
you're doing what you're doing.

00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:53.669
From a presentation
standpoint of PowerPoint,

00:04:53.669 --> 00:04:55.210
I think one of the
big takeaways here

00:04:55.210 --> 00:04:56.830
is that you're
just going to spend

00:04:56.830 --> 00:04:59.314
a lot less time iterating
on presentation slides

00:04:59.314 --> 00:05:00.730
and a lot more
time doing what you

00:05:00.730 --> 00:05:02.560
do best, which is data science.

00:05:06.110 --> 00:05:07.000
So how does it work?

00:05:10.280 --> 00:05:14.570
Well, you write your code
in R Markdown format.

00:05:14.570 --> 00:05:18.130
And then you knit the code
with the knitter package.

00:05:18.130 --> 00:05:21.340
And what knitter does is it
takes your R Markdown code,

00:05:21.340 --> 00:05:25.870
and it converts it
into Markdown format.

00:05:25.870 --> 00:05:28.840
And Markdown format
is a universal format

00:05:28.840 --> 00:05:32.200
that can be put into this
other system called Pandoc.

00:05:32.200 --> 00:05:35.890
And Pandoc is what actually
converts the Markdown document

00:05:35.890 --> 00:05:37.300
into PowerPoint.

00:05:37.300 --> 00:05:39.610
Now all of this is already
bundled into the IDE,

00:05:39.610 --> 00:05:41.920
so it's seamless,
but this is what's

00:05:41.920 --> 00:05:43.750
happening under the covers.

00:05:43.750 --> 00:05:46.970
So I want to focus on this
Pandoc piece for a second.

00:05:46.970 --> 00:05:50.020
What is Pandoc?

00:05:50.020 --> 00:05:52.720
Well, Pandoc is the open source
software that converts files

00:05:52.720 --> 00:05:54.080
from one format to another.

00:05:54.080 --> 00:05:56.230
You can think of as
a Swiss army knife.

00:05:56.230 --> 00:06:01.030
And PowerPoint output was added
to Pandoc and Pandoc version 2.

00:06:01.030 --> 00:06:03.820
The initial release
was in December 2017,

00:06:03.820 --> 00:06:06.340
and then there was a number
of progressive updates

00:06:06.340 --> 00:06:08.350
that happened at the
beginning of this year

00:06:08.350 --> 00:06:10.740
to allow you to create these--

00:06:10.740 --> 00:06:12.610
this PowerPoint output.

00:06:12.610 --> 00:06:17.410
And Pandoc is bundled
into the RStudio IDE.

00:06:17.410 --> 00:06:21.310
And the RStudio IDE
version 1.2 has the ability

00:06:21.310 --> 00:06:24.460
to create PowerPoint documents
in RStudio Connect as

00:06:24.460 --> 00:06:28.390
of version 1.6.4, which came
out a few months ago that also

00:06:28.390 --> 00:06:32.910
has the ability to support
PowerPoint documents.

00:06:32.910 --> 00:06:36.700
Now a lot of this work
here with Pandoc was done

00:06:36.700 --> 00:06:40.120
by a developer-- a
Haskell developer--

00:06:40.120 --> 00:06:46.020
Pandoc's written Haskell--
and he works out of--

00:06:46.020 --> 00:06:48.230
he's a professor at
Johns Hopkins University,

00:06:48.230 --> 00:06:49.956
and his name's Jesse Rosenthal.

00:06:49.956 --> 00:06:51.580
And Jesse's actually
on the call today,

00:06:51.580 --> 00:06:55.090
so it's great to have him
here and join in the webinar.

00:06:55.090 --> 00:06:57.880
But most of this
development work was--

00:06:57.880 --> 00:07:01.450
that allows us to convert
documents into PowerPoint

00:07:01.450 --> 00:07:02.470
was done by Jesse.

00:07:06.180 --> 00:07:11.170
R Markdown's not the only
way to do document conversion

00:07:11.170 --> 00:07:13.630
into PowerPoint.

00:07:13.630 --> 00:07:16.750
You have these other R packages
that you might have used,

00:07:16.750 --> 00:07:18.190
and these are great R packages.

00:07:18.190 --> 00:07:19.780
I've use some of them.

00:07:19.780 --> 00:07:22.810
They allow you to have fine
grain control over what

00:07:22.810 --> 00:07:24.490
happens on those slides.

00:07:24.490 --> 00:07:26.530
And some of these
will have features

00:07:26.530 --> 00:07:31.210
that aren't available
in the Markdown format--

00:07:31.210 --> 00:07:32.440
conversion format.

00:07:32.440 --> 00:07:34.776
So these packages
are still relevant.

00:07:34.776 --> 00:07:36.400
You might still find
these very useful.

00:07:39.900 --> 00:07:44.300
Let's talk about use cases
for creating PowerPoint

00:07:44.300 --> 00:07:45.725
presentations from R Markdown.

00:07:49.300 --> 00:07:51.910
The first one is to
render the presentation

00:07:51.910 --> 00:07:54.680
and then customize
the presentation.

00:07:54.680 --> 00:07:58.270
And that's probably going
to be a very common use case

00:07:58.270 --> 00:07:59.440
for your PowerPoint slides.

00:07:59.440 --> 00:08:01.891
You're going to output the
text, the tables, and the plots

00:08:01.891 --> 00:08:04.390
to PowerPoint, and then you're
in and modify these or copies

00:08:04.390 --> 00:08:06.650
into your final presentation.

00:08:06.650 --> 00:08:07.770
I'll give you an example.

00:08:12.630 --> 00:08:17.210
This is a notebook that
analyzes bank data,

00:08:17.210 --> 00:08:18.770
and in the course
of this analysis,

00:08:18.770 --> 00:08:21.680
I create a whole bunch
of visualizations

00:08:21.680 --> 00:08:23.540
that are interesting.

00:08:23.540 --> 00:08:25.790
And normally what I would
do with these visualizations

00:08:25.790 --> 00:08:31.220
is I would take these and
I'd copy these one at a time

00:08:31.220 --> 00:08:34.520
into my PowerPoint presentation.

00:08:34.520 --> 00:08:36.574
My PowerPoint presentation
would become a mess,

00:08:36.574 --> 00:08:38.574
and then I'd lose track
of which ones are which.

00:08:38.574 --> 00:08:40.400
And I'd have to go back and up--

00:08:40.400 --> 00:08:42.440
when I go back to
update my analysis,

00:08:42.440 --> 00:08:44.870
then things become out of sync.

00:08:44.870 --> 00:08:47.360
Be much better just
to build a render

00:08:47.360 --> 00:08:50.990
all the slides in one
shot from this notebook,

00:08:50.990 --> 00:08:54.710
and that's what you can do
with the PowerPoint output.

00:08:54.710 --> 00:08:59.285
So I come up here and say
knit this presentation.

00:08:59.285 --> 00:09:03.560
This will go through and
spin up a new R session,

00:09:03.560 --> 00:09:06.464
run all of the R code
through that session,

00:09:06.464 --> 00:09:08.297
and then generate a
PowerPoint presentation.

00:09:13.180 --> 00:09:15.490
So here's the slides.

00:09:15.490 --> 00:09:19.300
You can see that I've
got my narrative again.

00:09:19.300 --> 00:09:22.464
And then I've got
some graphs here.

00:09:22.464 --> 00:09:24.505
And I can take these
graphs, and I can copy these

00:09:24.505 --> 00:09:25.750
into my final presentation.

00:09:25.750 --> 00:09:28.740
I've got a table, and
that's really great, too.

00:09:28.740 --> 00:09:31.420
And I can-- if I don't
like the order here,

00:09:31.420 --> 00:09:34.820
I can just movies
around if I need to.

00:09:34.820 --> 00:09:37.370
But I don't like-- if I want
to change the background,

00:09:37.370 --> 00:09:39.650
change my template, I
can do that as well.

00:09:42.600 --> 00:09:45.090
Choose a different template.

00:09:45.090 --> 00:09:49.440
If I'm not happy with
the table, the table

00:09:49.440 --> 00:09:51.480
renders as a PowerPoint
table, so I can go ahead

00:09:51.480 --> 00:09:52.770
and change that table design.

00:09:55.680 --> 00:09:58.290
And I can-- and at this--
and I can come in here

00:09:58.290 --> 00:10:01.830
and say I've got more
than one data source.

00:10:01.830 --> 00:10:02.980
I've got data sources.

00:10:02.980 --> 00:10:05.980
I'm going to change that.

00:10:05.980 --> 00:10:08.970
And now I've really broken
the reproducibility paradigm.

00:10:08.970 --> 00:10:11.350
I've actually gone through
and made some changes.

00:10:11.350 --> 00:10:13.710
And now if I go back
and re-do the slides,

00:10:13.710 --> 00:10:15.360
I'm going to have
to do those again.

00:10:15.360 --> 00:10:18.150
But I'd argue that you're
much better off because you

00:10:18.150 --> 00:10:21.930
can produce a lot of
these at the same time,

00:10:21.930 --> 00:10:24.690
and you can keep them
anchored to that R Markdown

00:10:24.690 --> 00:10:27.477
document that explains
what you're doing.

00:10:27.477 --> 00:10:29.310
So we think this will
be a pretty common use

00:10:29.310 --> 00:10:32.250
case for taking slides and
then manipulating them.

00:10:32.250 --> 00:10:35.370
People like to customize their
PowerPoint presentations.

00:10:35.370 --> 00:10:36.990
And chances are your
slides are going

00:10:36.990 --> 00:10:40.920
to be one part of a larger
presentation that ends up

00:10:40.920 --> 00:10:45.030
getting delivered to the
executive or the client

00:10:45.030 --> 00:10:47.009
or whoever you're trying
to communicate with.

00:10:47.009 --> 00:10:48.800
And that will work
great in this work flow.

00:11:00.720 --> 00:11:03.720
The other use case is to
publish to RStudio Connect.

00:11:03.720 --> 00:11:04.720
What is RStudio Connect?

00:11:04.720 --> 00:11:08.350
It's a publishing platform
for R. It runs your R code,

00:11:08.350 --> 00:11:11.370
and it allows you to share your
shiny apps, R Markdown reports,

00:11:11.370 --> 00:11:13.047
and much, much more.

00:11:13.047 --> 00:11:14.880
So in this use case,
what you're going to do

00:11:14.880 --> 00:11:18.390
is you're going to output the
entire presentation with R

00:11:18.390 --> 00:11:21.000
Markdown, and then you're
going to render updates

00:11:21.000 --> 00:11:23.130
programmatically on a schedule.

00:11:23.130 --> 00:11:25.710
And then you'll distribute
those PowerPoint presentations

00:11:25.710 --> 00:11:27.570
by email, and you
can even design

00:11:27.570 --> 00:11:29.440
them to accept user inputs.

00:11:29.440 --> 00:11:32.450
So this is a little bit easier
to show than to explain.

00:11:36.350 --> 00:11:38.285
Here's another presentation
that I'm using.

00:11:38.285 --> 00:11:41.030
It's the stock data.

00:11:41.030 --> 00:11:43.560
And this is a parameters
R Markdown document.

00:11:43.560 --> 00:11:46.400
And if you haven't seen
parameters R Markdown before,

00:11:46.400 --> 00:11:47.540
they're extremely powerful.

00:11:47.540 --> 00:11:50.600
You set up parameters
here and then inputs here.

00:11:50.600 --> 00:11:55.000
This is going to be the
Tesla stock ticker symbol.

00:11:55.000 --> 00:11:58.070
And if I want to call that
in my R markdown document,

00:11:58.070 --> 00:12:00.980
I just use param string symbol.

00:12:00.980 --> 00:12:03.500
So these become very
useful in their own right

00:12:03.500 --> 00:12:07.100
even regardless of how
your accepting user

00:12:07.100 --> 00:12:08.870
inputs or what
output formats you're

00:12:08.870 --> 00:12:11.360
using parameters R Markdown.

00:12:11.360 --> 00:12:14.480
Parameterization is extremely
powerful in R Markdown

00:12:14.480 --> 00:12:15.640
documents.

00:12:15.640 --> 00:12:17.180
But here what I
can do is I can say

00:12:17.180 --> 00:12:18.500
I'm going to knit this thing.

00:12:18.500 --> 00:12:20.630
I'll go ahead and knit
it with parameters.

00:12:20.630 --> 00:12:23.840
And I'll choose the
IBM report I guess.

00:12:23.840 --> 00:12:24.920
I'll knit this document.

00:12:32.731 --> 00:12:33.230
Great.

00:12:33.230 --> 00:12:38.330
Now I have a document for
the IBM candlestick chart.

00:12:38.330 --> 00:12:40.600
And this is what I want,
but I can't anticipate

00:12:40.600 --> 00:12:46.480
everybody's needs here with all
of the different stock tickers

00:12:46.480 --> 00:12:48.370
that they might need.

00:12:48.370 --> 00:12:52.227
Nor am I able to
automate this every day.

00:12:52.227 --> 00:12:54.310
I don't have the ability
to update this every day.

00:12:54.310 --> 00:12:57.067
I have to be able to
automate it with the system.

00:12:57.067 --> 00:12:58.900
So I want to make sure
that this report gets

00:12:58.900 --> 00:13:01.510
generated daily and sent
out to the people that

00:13:01.510 --> 00:13:03.540
are interested in it.

00:13:03.540 --> 00:13:06.244
So what I do is I publish
this to RStudio Connect.

00:13:06.244 --> 00:13:08.035
And I can do that with
this Publish button.

00:13:08.035 --> 00:13:11.150
I choose RStudio
Connect, and this is what

00:13:11.150 --> 00:13:12.410
RStudio Connect books like.

00:13:17.920 --> 00:13:19.630
Here's the default report.

00:13:19.630 --> 00:13:21.160
That's going to be
the Tesla report.

00:13:21.160 --> 00:13:25.750
If I want to change the report
ticker, I can go in here

00:13:25.750 --> 00:13:29.200
and I can say I
want to change IBM.

00:13:29.200 --> 00:13:30.470
And you could run that report.

00:13:30.470 --> 00:13:31.930
I'm not going to run the
report because I've already

00:13:31.930 --> 00:13:33.250
done it for you.

00:13:33.250 --> 00:13:34.722
It's right here.

00:13:34.722 --> 00:13:37.180
And then if I want to, I can
go ahead and email this report

00:13:37.180 --> 00:13:42.120
to myself, or I come in here
and I can schedule this report

00:13:42.120 --> 00:13:47.300
to run every day and then send
me an email when it's complete.

00:13:47.300 --> 00:13:50.336
And I can add more
people to this report.

00:13:50.336 --> 00:13:51.710
And that-- what
that report looks

00:13:51.710 --> 00:13:57.610
like is if I go to my inbox, can
see that I just got the report.

00:13:57.610 --> 00:14:00.190
It's got this nice
graphic here that tells me

00:14:00.190 --> 00:14:02.465
that IBM closed at 120.03.

00:14:02.465 --> 00:14:04.120
Then I come down
here to the end,

00:14:04.120 --> 00:14:06.910
and I can see that I've
attached the PowerPoint

00:14:06.910 --> 00:14:11.170
presentation to the email.

00:14:11.170 --> 00:14:14.750
And again this is going to be
the updated report that I just

00:14:14.750 --> 00:14:16.545
ran.

00:14:16.545 --> 00:14:18.670
So you can see that this
would be a very useful way

00:14:18.670 --> 00:14:22.990
to communicate your insights
with a lot of people

00:14:22.990 --> 00:14:26.770
who prefer to get their
information by email

00:14:26.770 --> 00:14:28.126
and PowerPoint.

00:14:28.126 --> 00:14:29.500
I personally know
a lot of people

00:14:29.500 --> 00:14:30.958
in that boat that
would like to get

00:14:30.958 --> 00:14:33.830
their information
communicated to them in email

00:14:33.830 --> 00:14:34.780
and PowerPoint.

00:14:34.780 --> 00:14:36.860
Perhaps you do as well.

00:14:36.860 --> 00:14:38.920
The nice thing here
is that you only

00:14:38.920 --> 00:14:41.800
have to write the code once,
and then RStudio Connect

00:14:41.800 --> 00:14:47.000
can handle the scheduling
and the distribution

00:14:47.000 --> 00:14:50.529
and accept the user
inputs systematically.

00:14:57.520 --> 00:14:59.047
So those are some use cases.

00:14:59.047 --> 00:15:00.130
Let's talk about features.

00:15:03.630 --> 00:15:09.030
So the PowerPoint output accepts
most of the Markdown features

00:15:09.030 --> 00:15:12.210
that are supported in
other document formats.

00:15:12.210 --> 00:15:17.050
For example, inline
formatting is supported.

00:15:17.050 --> 00:15:21.370
So this is what the 9 formatting
looks like in your code,

00:15:21.370 --> 00:15:25.400
and this is how it gets
rendered into PowerPoint.

00:15:25.400 --> 00:15:26.075
Supports lists.

00:15:29.630 --> 00:15:30.900
LaTex.

00:15:30.900 --> 00:15:34.620
So if you're putting in
math equations, hyperlinks,

00:15:34.620 --> 00:15:41.930
of course, block quotes,
images from the web--

00:15:41.930 --> 00:15:44.180
this went out and snagged
an image from the web

00:15:44.180 --> 00:15:47.940
and then put it into my slide--

00:15:47.940 --> 00:15:48.780
images from file.

00:15:48.780 --> 00:15:50.790
This actually took
an image from a file

00:15:50.790 --> 00:15:53.280
and put it into my slide.

00:15:53.280 --> 00:15:56.800
You can add captions to images.

00:15:56.800 --> 00:15:59.820
You can add a link to
caption to the image.

00:15:59.820 --> 00:16:01.260
Or you can link the image.

00:16:01.260 --> 00:16:04.240
In this case, what happens
is if I click this image,

00:16:04.240 --> 00:16:08.550
this will take me to the
R for Data Science page.

00:16:08.550 --> 00:16:09.750
Sports tables.

00:16:09.750 --> 00:16:12.030
So you write your
tables and Markdown code

00:16:12.030 --> 00:16:15.750
that looks like this, and
they get rendered like this.

00:16:15.750 --> 00:16:17.250
Notice there is a
caption down here.

00:16:19.836 --> 00:16:24.440
It also supports these really
nifty features like columns.

00:16:24.440 --> 00:16:25.710
So columns are great.

00:16:25.710 --> 00:16:28.790
What you do here is
you create these--

00:16:28.790 --> 00:16:32.150
this fence-- this
called a fence div--

00:16:32.150 --> 00:16:35.240
and you put all the contents
for the first column here.

00:16:35.240 --> 00:16:38.900
And you put all the contents
for the second column here.

00:16:38.900 --> 00:16:42.362
And then it will show up
in your two-column layout.

00:16:42.362 --> 00:16:43.820
It's called the
two-content layout.

00:16:43.820 --> 00:16:49.070
It's two columns and-- two
contents of two columns.

00:16:49.070 --> 00:16:51.050
It supports speaker notes.

00:16:51.050 --> 00:16:53.360
Speaker notes are very useful.

00:16:53.360 --> 00:16:55.130
Again, you put a
sensitive here, and you

00:16:55.130 --> 00:16:56.810
can put the speaker
notes that'll

00:16:56.810 --> 00:16:59.990
render in the presentation.

00:16:59.990 --> 00:17:01.380
And templates.

00:17:01.380 --> 00:17:03.230
The templates become
very useful also,

00:17:03.230 --> 00:17:05.480
and I will be talking about
that a little bit later.

00:17:11.000 --> 00:17:14.079
And, of course, this
translation supports R Code.

00:17:14.079 --> 00:17:15.400
That's the whole point of this.

00:17:15.400 --> 00:17:17.920
You want to be able to put
R Code into your R Markdown

00:17:17.920 --> 00:17:19.150
documents.

00:17:19.150 --> 00:17:21.339
What kind of R Code
does it support?

00:17:21.339 --> 00:17:24.069
Well, any of the R Code
that's in your R Code chunks

00:17:24.069 --> 00:17:27.250
and that typically is
going to look like code.

00:17:27.250 --> 00:17:30.910
And your code is--
gets highlighted.

00:17:30.910 --> 00:17:33.670
The syntax of your code gets
highlighted in the output

00:17:33.670 --> 00:17:36.880
but automatically.

00:17:36.880 --> 00:17:37.670
Tables.

00:17:37.670 --> 00:17:41.230
So if you call the cable
function, which is in knitter,

00:17:41.230 --> 00:17:45.410
it will create a
table in PowerPoint.

00:17:45.410 --> 00:17:47.600
GG plot or any
other visualization,

00:17:47.600 --> 00:17:51.150
it will render that
image on a content slide.

00:17:53.960 --> 00:17:57.830
And if you do something
interactive like an HTML

00:17:57.830 --> 00:18:01.320
widgets-- here I'm
using diagraphs--

00:18:01.320 --> 00:18:03.550
the rendering process will go--

00:18:03.550 --> 00:18:05.897
if you have installed
the web shot package,

00:18:05.897 --> 00:18:07.480
the rendering process
will go and grab

00:18:07.480 --> 00:18:12.280
a web screenshot of your
interactive visualization

00:18:12.280 --> 00:18:15.679
and include it in your
PowerPoint presentation.

00:18:19.810 --> 00:18:21.090
And finally Shiny.

00:18:21.090 --> 00:18:26.130
How do you include Shiny in
your PowerPoint presentations?

00:18:26.130 --> 00:18:28.080
Shiny's interactive.

00:18:28.080 --> 00:18:29.850
PowerPoint presentations
are static.

00:18:29.850 --> 00:18:32.520
So obviously you can't put
Shiny inside of higher point.

00:18:32.520 --> 00:18:34.194
That would be ridiculous.

00:18:34.194 --> 00:18:35.610
What you're going
to do instead is

00:18:35.610 --> 00:18:39.840
you're going to put a static
image of your Shiny application

00:18:39.840 --> 00:18:43.470
on your slide and then
link that to your Shiny

00:18:43.470 --> 00:18:45.330
application, which
will be hosted

00:18:45.330 --> 00:18:51.730
in Shiny app's I/O, RStudio
Connect, or Shiny server.

00:18:51.730 --> 00:18:54.190
So I will show you an example.

00:18:57.000 --> 00:19:01.150
In the course of my analysis, I
created this Shiny application,

00:19:01.150 --> 00:19:03.117
set customer tracker app.

00:19:03.117 --> 00:19:04.700
The nice thing about
this is it allows

00:19:04.700 --> 00:19:09.680
me to drill down into
subgroups and other segments.

00:19:09.680 --> 00:19:13.910
But obviously I can't put all
of those segments or groups

00:19:13.910 --> 00:19:16.410
into a PowerPoint presentation.

00:19:16.410 --> 00:19:17.190
So what do I do?

00:19:22.110 --> 00:19:26.440
I go back to the code, and I
include this line include app.

00:19:26.440 --> 00:19:29.600
And I put the URL
for that application

00:19:29.600 --> 00:19:33.110
into my presentation.

00:19:33.110 --> 00:19:35.000
Then when I knit
this process, it's

00:19:35.000 --> 00:19:37.970
going to go and take a
screenshot of that Shiny

00:19:37.970 --> 00:19:41.820
application and put it
back into my presentation.

00:19:41.820 --> 00:19:46.140
For example, this deck
shows the total segment.

00:19:46.140 --> 00:19:49.560
And the total group gives
me some high-level results

00:19:49.560 --> 00:19:51.030
and some details.

00:19:51.030 --> 00:19:55.660
And then it says use the Shiny
app to explore other segments

00:19:55.660 --> 00:19:57.250
and groups.

00:19:57.250 --> 00:20:01.070
And this image here
is a linked image.

00:20:01.070 --> 00:20:04.370
And I can click on
this image and it--

00:20:09.938 --> 00:20:13.665
I seem to have lost my
PowerPoint presentation.

00:20:13.665 --> 00:20:14.540
Let's try that again.

00:20:30.092 --> 00:20:31.550
So when I click on
this image, this

00:20:31.550 --> 00:20:34.127
will take me back to
my Shiny application.

00:20:37.950 --> 00:20:39.850
And this Shiny-- this
will allow your views--

00:20:39.850 --> 00:20:47.020
your viewers to interact
with your application.

00:20:47.020 --> 00:20:48.400
Choose a subgroup.

00:20:48.400 --> 00:20:50.470
And I've even put
a download link

00:20:50.470 --> 00:20:54.860
here to take a snapshot
of this report that

00:20:54.860 --> 00:20:57.770
is formatted in
Excel and allows you

00:20:57.770 --> 00:21:01.460
to see the report
that you've dug into.

00:21:01.460 --> 00:21:04.010
Gives you all the
data to do, cross tabs

00:21:04.010 --> 00:21:06.060
if you want to do that.

00:21:06.060 --> 00:21:11.350
But what I've done here is I've
tied PowerPoint presentation

00:21:11.350 --> 00:21:15.490
and Excel output and
a Shiny application

00:21:15.490 --> 00:21:19.930
and put them all on top
of the same code base.

00:21:36.380 --> 00:21:38.640
So let's talk about resources.

00:21:38.640 --> 00:21:42.906
What resources are available?

00:21:42.906 --> 00:21:44.280
The first place
to go to get help

00:21:44.280 --> 00:21:46.500
would probably R
Markdown to RStudio.com.

00:21:46.500 --> 00:21:49.370
That's our website
about R Markdown.

00:21:49.370 --> 00:21:51.646
There's also a new book
that's out called R Markdown--

00:21:51.646 --> 00:21:52.520
The Definitive Guide.

00:21:52.520 --> 00:21:57.200
It gives you information
about all of the output types

00:21:57.200 --> 00:22:00.740
and everything that you'd
want to know that R Markdown.

00:22:00.740 --> 00:22:03.800
I've created a Getting Started
page and a troubleshooting page

00:22:03.800 --> 00:22:05.930
for PowerPoint
output specifically,

00:22:05.930 --> 00:22:07.880
so I recommend taking
a look at those.

00:22:07.880 --> 00:22:10.520
If you have specific
questions about your work,

00:22:10.520 --> 00:22:13.100
then community.RStudio.com
is a great place

00:22:13.100 --> 00:22:14.840
to connect with other people.

00:22:14.840 --> 00:22:18.410
And then if you do find
any issues, please let us

00:22:18.410 --> 00:22:24.058
know by submitting issues to
the R Markdown GitHub repo.

00:22:24.058 --> 00:22:27.090
I want to address some
frequently asked questions.

00:22:27.090 --> 00:22:29.730
There is a question
about getting started

00:22:29.730 --> 00:22:32.370
about using templates
and presentations that we

00:22:32.370 --> 00:22:33.528
get frequently.

00:22:36.340 --> 00:22:38.070
So this is the
Getting Started page.

00:22:38.070 --> 00:22:42.250
If I come down
here to templates,

00:22:42.250 --> 00:22:46.060
you can see that the way
you set up a template

00:22:46.060 --> 00:22:49.950
is with this reference doc.

00:22:49.950 --> 00:22:54.600
And the template supports
four of the many layouts

00:22:54.600 --> 00:22:56.250
that are in an RStudio--

00:22:56.250 --> 00:22:58.800
or in a PowerPoint template.

00:22:58.800 --> 00:23:00.900
These are the
layouts that you want

00:23:00.900 --> 00:23:06.700
to include in your template
for R Markdown documents.

00:23:06.700 --> 00:23:09.480
The first one is
the title layout.

00:23:09.480 --> 00:23:14.100
So this will give you the title
and the subtitle on this slide.

00:23:14.100 --> 00:23:16.980
The next one is
the section header.

00:23:16.980 --> 00:23:19.740
And the section header will
only render the content.

00:23:19.740 --> 00:23:21.270
It won't render--

00:23:21.270 --> 00:23:25.900
I'm sorry-- will only render
the header and not the content.

00:23:25.900 --> 00:23:28.280
The title and content
slide, which you typically

00:23:28.280 --> 00:23:32.150
think of as the main slide, and
then the two-content layout,

00:23:32.150 --> 00:23:34.910
which allows you to do columns.

00:23:34.910 --> 00:23:40.490
Now layouts are important
because Markdown doesn't allow

00:23:40.490 --> 00:23:45.380
you to programmatically
change the design

00:23:45.380 --> 00:23:48.620
or size of your presentation.

00:23:48.620 --> 00:23:51.330
So what it does is it actually--

00:23:51.330 --> 00:23:57.680
Pandoc actually ships with a
4 by 3 vanilla template that

00:23:57.680 --> 00:24:00.630
is used to render
presentations by default.

00:24:00.630 --> 00:24:04.310
So if you want to use
a widescreen format,

00:24:04.310 --> 00:24:07.580
you're going to have to
create a widescreen template

00:24:07.580 --> 00:24:11.000
and include that as a custom
template here in your reference

00:24:11.000 --> 00:24:11.770
doc.

00:24:11.770 --> 00:24:13.560
If you want to
change the background

00:24:13.560 --> 00:24:15.590
or if you want to
change the layout--

00:24:15.590 --> 00:24:18.470
the place holders
in any way, that's

00:24:18.470 --> 00:24:20.180
all going to happen
with the template.

00:24:24.742 --> 00:24:26.200
The next thing I
want to talk about

00:24:26.200 --> 00:24:28.470
is structuring the presentation.

00:24:28.470 --> 00:24:31.330
So a PowerPoint present--
this gets little detailed,

00:24:31.330 --> 00:24:33.820
but I think it's
important to go through.

00:24:33.820 --> 00:24:35.800
PowerPoint presentations
have a hierarchy

00:24:35.800 --> 00:24:39.790
of title, section, and content
whereas markdown documents have

00:24:39.790 --> 00:24:42.490
a hierarchy of
headers and contents.

00:24:42.490 --> 00:24:46.090
So in order for the PowerPoint
presentation to get structured,

00:24:46.090 --> 00:24:49.000
Pandoc has to determine which
markdown header level should

00:24:49.000 --> 00:24:51.290
be used for the
PowerPoint slide level.

00:24:51.290 --> 00:24:58.070
So, for example, in
this Markdown document,

00:24:58.070 --> 00:25:03.820
you have a hierarchy of
headers that go from 1 to 5.

00:25:03.820 --> 00:25:07.420
And this header is
the one that Pandoc

00:25:07.420 --> 00:25:10.980
is going to use as the
slide level by default.

00:25:10.980 --> 00:25:14.970
And it's going to use
it as a slide level

00:25:14.970 --> 00:25:17.370
because it's the
first slide that

00:25:17.370 --> 00:25:20.380
has content associated with it.

00:25:20.380 --> 00:25:22.380
So everything that gets
rendered above this

00:25:22.380 --> 00:25:24.720
is going to be turn
into sections--

00:25:24.720 --> 00:25:26.520
section slides, and
everything below this

00:25:26.520 --> 00:25:30.420
is going to be turned into sub
headers on the content slide.

00:25:30.420 --> 00:25:32.760
Now if you didn't want this
to be the content slide

00:25:32.760 --> 00:25:35.910
and you wanted this
to be the slide level,

00:25:35.910 --> 00:25:40.980
you would change slide level
to 2 because 2 would mesh--

00:25:40.980 --> 00:25:46.680
would match up to the two
hashtags on this R Markdown

00:25:46.680 --> 00:25:48.850
section header.

00:25:48.850 --> 00:25:51.370
So you probably don't have
to worry too much about this

00:25:51.370 --> 00:25:56.510
because the defaults should
work well for you in most cases.

00:25:56.510 --> 00:25:59.690
If you want to avoid
this issue altogether,

00:25:59.690 --> 00:26:03.340
you can just use the same
level header for everything,

00:26:03.340 --> 00:26:06.250
and then everything will be
rendered as content slides.

00:26:08.840 --> 00:26:11.900
The way that the
slides get broken up,

00:26:11.900 --> 00:26:13.795
follows these five rules.

00:26:13.795 --> 00:26:16.040
I'm not going to go through
all these right now.

00:26:16.040 --> 00:26:19.420
But just know that
there is a way to--

00:26:19.420 --> 00:26:21.470
that they get
rendered and there's

00:26:21.470 --> 00:26:23.600
an easy way to find out
what those rules are.

00:26:23.600 --> 00:26:27.203
Just go to the Getting Started
page and it will explain it.

00:26:31.260 --> 00:26:33.390
So troubleshooting.

00:26:33.390 --> 00:26:37.790
What types of issues might
you expect to run into?

00:26:37.790 --> 00:26:40.760
Well, some of the common
things that we see

00:26:40.760 --> 00:26:44.360
is that the PowerPoint
presentation gets broken

00:26:44.360 --> 00:26:46.550
and you get a message
that says that it needs

00:26:46.550 --> 00:26:48.920
to be repaired or removed.

00:26:48.920 --> 00:26:51.050
Or you get this weird
one, which doesn't even

00:26:51.050 --> 00:26:52.740
give you anything at all.

00:26:52.740 --> 00:26:56.420
And typically the
reason for these errors

00:26:56.420 --> 00:26:59.170
is that the Markdown
code that you wrote

00:26:59.170 --> 00:27:01.790
looks something like
this where everything

00:27:01.790 --> 00:27:03.630
is condensed together.

00:27:03.630 --> 00:27:05.186
And when you write R--

00:27:05.186 --> 00:27:06.560
when you write
Markdown code, you

00:27:06.560 --> 00:27:10.250
need to put spaces in between
the content, the title,

00:27:10.250 --> 00:27:13.170
and the speaker notes.

00:27:13.170 --> 00:27:17.450
And if you go and put those
spaces back in and then

00:27:17.450 --> 00:27:20.610
restart PowerPoint--

00:27:20.610 --> 00:27:22.610
PowerPoint's an
old program, needs

00:27:22.610 --> 00:27:25.820
to be restarted every time you
get into this weird state--

00:27:25.820 --> 00:27:30.470
if you go back, put the
spaces, and restart PowerPoint,

00:27:30.470 --> 00:27:32.360
this should read render
more successfully.

00:27:32.360 --> 00:27:34.026
So that would be the
first thing to try.

00:27:36.440 --> 00:27:39.230
How do I put an image in
a text on the same slide?

00:27:39.230 --> 00:27:42.870
There's two ways to put images
and text on the same slide.

00:27:42.870 --> 00:27:44.830
One is to use captions.

00:27:44.830 --> 00:27:46.990
And if you use a
caption in Markdown,

00:27:46.990 --> 00:27:49.000
it's going to look
something like this.

00:27:49.000 --> 00:27:51.950
And if you use caption
in a code junk,

00:27:51.950 --> 00:27:54.290
it's going to look
something like this.

00:27:54.290 --> 00:27:56.320
And you can write very
long captions here.

00:27:56.320 --> 00:27:59.980
So this is actually a very
workable solution, probably

00:27:59.980 --> 00:28:04.160
the easiest solution
to your code.

00:28:04.160 --> 00:28:07.130
If you-- and table
support captions as well,

00:28:07.130 --> 00:28:10.040
you have to use table
caption on those.

00:28:10.040 --> 00:28:14.420
So the other way to do
it is to use columns.

00:28:14.420 --> 00:28:16.430
And this uses the
two-content layout

00:28:16.430 --> 00:28:20.170
that you could put your text
on one side and your image

00:28:20.170 --> 00:28:21.100
on the other side.

00:28:21.100 --> 00:28:24.290
You could actually add a
caption to this image as well.

00:28:24.290 --> 00:28:29.080
So this is a great way to
separate text and columns.

00:28:29.080 --> 00:28:31.780
You could put images on
both columns or an image

00:28:31.780 --> 00:28:35.247
on the side and text
on the other side.

00:28:35.247 --> 00:28:36.830
But then the next
question, of course,

00:28:36.830 --> 00:28:38.496
is, well, how do I
control the placement

00:28:38.496 --> 00:28:40.540
of the images and text?

00:28:40.540 --> 00:28:44.920
And there is no way to
do that programmatically.

00:28:44.920 --> 00:28:47.290
You have to do that
with the templates

00:28:47.290 --> 00:28:48.980
as I mentioned earlier.

00:28:48.980 --> 00:28:54.170
So here what I've done is I've
taken the two-content layout

00:28:54.170 --> 00:28:58.760
in my template, and I've stacked
one of the content placeholders

00:28:58.760 --> 00:29:00.200
on top of the other one.

00:29:00.200 --> 00:29:02.180
And it renders
quite nicely, allows

00:29:02.180 --> 00:29:07.460
me to put an image on the
bottom and text on the top.

00:29:07.460 --> 00:29:10.280
The only issue here is that
you only get one of these,

00:29:10.280 --> 00:29:12.260
so you have to stick
with this layout

00:29:12.260 --> 00:29:14.030
throughout your presentation.

00:29:14.030 --> 00:29:18.470
But this will work really
nicely for this layout.

00:29:18.470 --> 00:29:21.350
If you need more
programmatic control,

00:29:21.350 --> 00:29:23.427
then you're probably going
to be in the situation

00:29:23.427 --> 00:29:25.010
where you need one
of those R packages

00:29:25.010 --> 00:29:26.240
we talked about earlier.

00:29:28.780 --> 00:29:31.420
How do I control the
size of an image?

00:29:31.420 --> 00:29:33.190
Well, you can't.

00:29:33.190 --> 00:29:37.450
Pandoc automatically resizes
images to fit a placeholder.

00:29:37.450 --> 00:29:40.180
Currently it's not possible
programmatically change image

00:29:40.180 --> 00:29:41.860
size.

00:29:41.860 --> 00:29:43.472
How do I create a build slide?

00:29:43.472 --> 00:29:44.680
Build slides are very useful.

00:29:44.680 --> 00:29:46.340
Currently that's not supported.

00:29:46.340 --> 00:29:48.460
So you can't do a
build slide today.

00:29:48.460 --> 00:29:51.415
That's where you click the
button and bullet point 1, 2,

00:29:51.415 --> 00:29:54.170
3 show up in order.

00:29:54.170 --> 00:29:56.720
How do I render a
presentation programmatically?

00:29:56.720 --> 00:30:00.190
We talked about that already
through the render command.

00:30:00.190 --> 00:30:01.750
And how do I check
my Markdown code.

00:30:01.750 --> 00:30:03.708
So sometimes it's useful
to check your Markdown

00:30:03.708 --> 00:30:06.370
code if you're trying to
debug your rendering process.

00:30:06.370 --> 00:30:08.530
And the way you
do that is you use

00:30:08.530 --> 00:30:11.050
the clean equals
false option, and that

00:30:11.050 --> 00:30:13.990
will output the
Markdown file that gets

00:30:13.990 --> 00:30:15.820
generated in the
rendering process.

00:30:15.820 --> 00:30:19.120
You can open that up and see
if it's creating the Markdown

00:30:19.120 --> 00:30:20.650
that you intended to create.

00:30:26.580 --> 00:30:28.890
So all of the things
that you saw today

00:30:28.890 --> 00:30:33.086
were generated in R Markdown
with the PowerPoint output

00:30:33.086 --> 00:30:35.460
including this presentation
that you're looking at today.

00:30:35.460 --> 00:30:38.430
This was also generated
from an R Markdown document.

00:30:38.430 --> 00:30:41.220
And all of that is
stored in a GitHub repo

00:30:41.220 --> 00:30:45.480
under the Soledge category as--

00:30:45.480 --> 00:30:48.060
with-- and it's
called PowerPoint.

00:30:48.060 --> 00:30:50.310
I've included a number
of vignettes in here

00:30:50.310 --> 00:30:52.410
as well that I didn't
show today but are

00:30:52.410 --> 00:30:57.180
very useful in understanding
how these features work.

00:30:57.180 --> 00:30:59.140
If you're interested
in looking at that,

00:30:59.140 --> 00:31:03.070
you can download the GitHub
repo today and try it out.

00:31:03.070 --> 00:31:08.040
But make sure that you install
R Studio version 1.2 preview.

00:31:08.040 --> 00:31:11.440
And you can get there by
googling RStudio Preview,

00:31:11.440 --> 00:31:16.085
downloading the latest copy,
and trying this out today.

00:31:16.085 --> 00:31:18.710
So I hope you have a lot of fun
making PowerPoint presentations

00:31:18.710 --> 00:31:20.460
with R Markdown.

00:31:20.460 --> 00:31:22.761
And thank you very
much for the time.

00:31:22.761 --> 00:31:25.010
I'll-- let me toggle over
and take some questions now.

00:31:28.390 --> 00:31:35.056
What customizations of
PowerPoint can you do within R?

00:31:35.056 --> 00:31:37.290
I think the best--

00:31:37.290 --> 00:31:40.490
can you do any
customizations on tables?

00:31:40.490 --> 00:31:43.620
Yeah, so that's all
going to be dictated--

00:31:43.620 --> 00:31:47.460
I think the question is can
I programmatically change

00:31:47.460 --> 00:31:50.640
the table color
or shape or size.

00:31:50.640 --> 00:31:55.120
All of that is going to be
dictated by the templates.

00:31:55.120 --> 00:31:56.850
So you're going
to want to define

00:31:56.850 --> 00:31:58.060
all of that in the template.

00:31:58.060 --> 00:31:59.730
So if you want to
change the link color,

00:31:59.730 --> 00:32:01.625
for example, you'll
change the color

00:32:01.625 --> 00:32:04.730
of the link in the
template and then

00:32:04.730 --> 00:32:08.370
Pandoc will automatically render
those links in that color.

00:32:11.765 --> 00:32:19.030
Are those RMD codes embedded
in the PowerPoint file?

00:32:19.030 --> 00:32:22.670
The RMD codes embedded
in the PowerPoint file.

00:32:22.670 --> 00:32:24.200
If I understand
correctly, you're

00:32:24.200 --> 00:32:27.950
talking about the
syntax highlighting

00:32:27.950 --> 00:32:33.870
and how the code is being
outputted to the PowerPoint

00:32:33.870 --> 00:32:34.560
file.

00:32:34.560 --> 00:32:36.286
That's in the echo.

00:32:36.286 --> 00:32:38.160
But I'm a little confused
about this question

00:32:38.160 --> 00:32:42.242
because you won't actually
have code in the PowerPoint.

00:32:42.242 --> 00:32:44.700
There's no code that's going
to run in the PowerPoint file.

00:32:44.700 --> 00:32:47.580
You're just going to echo
the code into the PowerPoint

00:32:47.580 --> 00:32:52.950
slides, and Pandoc is going
to do the syntax highlighting

00:32:52.950 --> 00:32:53.956
for you.

00:32:53.956 --> 00:32:55.330
I hope that answers
the question.

00:32:55.330 --> 00:32:57.510
If not, let me know.

00:32:57.510 --> 00:33:04.940
Can you trigger an email
based on output in R Connect?

00:33:04.940 --> 00:33:07.700
So the emails are--

00:33:07.700 --> 00:33:10.530
you can manually email yourself.

00:33:10.530 --> 00:33:12.120
Let me just showed this again.

00:33:15.430 --> 00:33:17.740
So the way you
email in PowerPoint

00:33:17.740 --> 00:33:21.100
is you can email yourself here.

00:33:21.100 --> 00:33:22.870
That's one way to email.

00:33:22.870 --> 00:33:26.260
The other way to email
is to send an email

00:33:26.260 --> 00:33:29.440
after it has been rendered--

00:33:29.440 --> 00:33:31.540
after it's been completed--

00:33:31.540 --> 00:33:34.089
after it's completed
the schedule.

00:33:34.089 --> 00:33:34.880
So that's the way--

00:33:34.880 --> 00:33:37.460
so you can-- basically
you can send emails either

00:33:37.460 --> 00:33:39.870
on demand or programmatically.

00:33:39.870 --> 00:33:42.900
Good question.

00:33:42.900 --> 00:33:45.320
Does it support
biv tech citations

00:33:45.320 --> 00:33:48.370
as a footnote of the slide.

00:33:48.370 --> 00:33:52.560
So the-- I don't
think I've tried that.

00:33:52.560 --> 00:33:55.030
That's a good question.

00:33:55.030 --> 00:33:56.340
I haven't tried that.

00:33:56.340 --> 00:34:00.360
There are some footnotes
that belong to the layouts,

00:34:00.360 --> 00:34:03.280
and you might have seen
